---
import ActionBracket024 from "../../../components/studies/ActionBracket024.svelte";
import ActionBracketTone025 from "../../../components/studies/ActionBracketTone025.svelte";
import ActionEdge023 from "../../../components/studies/ActionEdge023.svelte";
import ActionFocus028 from "../../../components/studies/ActionFocus028.svelte";
import ActionHeld027 from "../../../components/studies/ActionHeld027.svelte";
import ActionLinkLine0213 from "../../../components/studies/ActionLinkLine0213.svelte";
import ActionLinks0212 from "../../../components/studies/ActionLinks0212.svelte";
import ActionMaterials021 from "../../../components/studies/ActionMaterials021.svelte";
import ActionPointer026 from "../../../components/studies/ActionPointer026.svelte";
import ActionRest022 from "../../../components/studies/ActionRest022.svelte";
import ActionSheet0214 from "../../../components/studies/ActionSheet0214.svelte";
import ActionStrip0211 from "../../../components/studies/ActionStrip0211.svelte";
import ActionTogether0210 from "../../../components/studies/ActionTogether0210.svelte";
import ActionUnavailable029 from "../../../components/studies/ActionUnavailable029.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "buttons-2026-11-11");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / One lit action among quiet controls</h2>
    <p>
      The interface is meant to read as one console of lit, almost holographic
      controls. The design system already names its surfaces console and
      display; the holographic reading is this study's and is not yet in the
      design principles. An action is what a player presses to make something
      happen or to go somewhere: a button or a link. On
      <a href="/">the title screen</a>
      a filled lime button says Press start, and the four actions beneath it
      each wear a full lime outline. In
      <a href="/hangar">the Hangar</a>
      the launch row sets three outlined buttons beside the filled Survival, on
      the same screen as ship tabs, a toggle and switches that are made
      differently: cut surfaces raised above their background, with no outline
      while available and a lime fill only on what is selected. A pointer tints
      a tab or a toggle part with faint lime and brightens a switch's track. The
      actions predate that language. They read as shapes drawn on a page; the
      newer controls read as panes of the console.
    </p>
    <p>
      The outline also spends lime on every action at once. Five lime shapes in
      a column leave the call to action only a little ahead, and a pointer fills
      an outlined button solid lime, so for a moment it is the call to action.
      Keyboard focus draws a second, pale ring inside the first outline. An
      unavailable button is the same outline at lower opacity, which in a row
      still reads as an offer. Every action that leads to another page is
      dressed as a button; the game has almost no text links, so there is no
      quieter way to offer one. Yet lime is what says a thing can be acted on. A
      quieter action must stay recognisably pressable, and must not be taken for
      a tab, a toggle part or a label: those choose and inform, an action
      commands.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Fixed</dt>
      <dd>
        The filled lime call to action, at rest and under a pointer. The
        top-left and bottom-right cut corners of a button. The 44px minimum
        target. Which actions each screen offers, and their order.
      </dd>
      <dt>Open: the action</dt>
      <dd>
        What the quiet button and the text link are made of at rest and under a
        pointer, and what every action, the call to action included, shows when
        held down, focused from the keyboard and unavailable. Held down has no
        treatment today; focus and unavailable are each one rule shared by every
        button. A third shared rule marks a button selected with a lime bar
        along its bottom edge, used in the game only by the Leaderboard's sort
        pair, where the selected button is also filled lime and the bar cannot
        be seen; whether an action keeps a selected state at all is open. The
        newer controls do not agree on unavailable: a tab becomes an empty
        frame, a toggle part and a switch fade.
      </dd>
      <dt>Open: the action among others</dt>
      <dd>
        An action is almost never seen alone, so how it looks beside other
        actions is part of what it is made of: how the call to action leads the
        quiet ones, which actions are buttons and which are links, how an
        unavailable one sits in a row, and the surface, gaps and widths that
        make a set read as one piece. This is one question with the material,
        not a second one. Today the title screen, pause and run complete share a
        vertical group on a panel, where each outlined button's interior is the
        void; Settings and the Leaderboard share a horizontal strip that paints
        its own void surface, on the Leaderboard inside a panel; the Hangar's
        launch row is built separately, on the void, with its own spacing and
        widths. Remove fitting, the Credits return link and the filled Return to
        Hangar on the flight-unavailable dialog stand alone. On Settings,
        Credits and an unranked run complete there is no call to action, so a
        quiet action is the only one offered.
      </dd>
      <dt>Links today</dt>
      <dd>
        The stylesheet has a link button: lime text without a cut or a surface,
        turning pale under a pointer. Only the Lab uses it. Links in study and
        Lab prose are lime. In the game the only links that are not dressed as
        buttons are the logomark in page headers and a fallback sentence on the
        page that redirects the old builder address, which a player normally
        never sees. The stylesheet also has a muted, caption-size link for
        flight dialogs that nothing uses.
      </dd>
      <dt>Reach</dt>
      <dd>
        The answer changes the shared outlined button and link button wherever
        they are used: the title menu, the Hangar's launch row and Remove
        fitting, the pause and run-complete dialogs, the return links on
        Settings and Credits, the Leaderboard's Refresh and the unselected half
        of its sort pair, and the Lab pages that use them. The shared state
        rules reach every button, the red one included, and the Lab buttons that
        carry no variant or one that has no rule. The answer is judged in the
        title screen's group and the Hangar's launch row. A text link, a
        selected button and the horizontal strip do not occur in those two
        places; each is shown in a built example beside them. From this study
        on, buttons and links are documented together on one canonical page,
        <code>/lab/ds/actions</code>; buttons leave <code>/lab/inputs</code>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Not actions</dt>
      <dd>
        The flight deck is its own instrument. Its Pause and Weapons controls
        and the thrust pads share the design system's tokens and nothing else
        with the standard actions; nothing here applies to them and they are not
        documented on the actions page. The Hangar's mount and part controls,
        like tabs, the toggle and the switch, are selection tools: they choose,
        they do not command.
      </dd>
      <dt>Adjacent</dt>
      <dd>
        The Leaderboard's sort pair is a choice built from two buttons, with the
        filled one meaning selected, so two filled buttons show there at once.
        Its look follows the answer; whether it should be a selection tool
        instead is a separate question. The design system lists a red button for
        consequences, but it exists only in the stylesheet and the Lab and no
        game screen uses it; it is not a given here and this study does not
        settle it.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer with no part in the framing tested this section
        against the question rubric as adjusted that day. It was the fourth run;
        the section and the rubric both changed after the first three. The
        reviewer found no false claim and listed six omissions and imprecisions.
        Each is now answered in the text above, which has not been tested again.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/buttons-2026-11-11">Full result and findings</a>.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Stamp</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              One object, the action that is not the call to action, as a quiet
              button and as a text link, in its states and among other actions.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists as described and no claim about the
              current interface is false.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              The shared outlined button and link button change wherever they
              are used, and both are documented together afterwards.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="materials">
      <summary>02.1 / Four materials for a quiet action</summary>
      <p>
        The first round asks the widest version of the question: what is a quiet
        action made of at all? Four different answers stand below today's
        outlined button, each in the title screen's group on a panel, in the
        Hangar's launch row on the void, and in a strip of its states. The call
        to action is the production button in every one.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What is held still.</strong>
          Each set keeps today's arrangement: the shared vertical group, and the
          Hangar row's own gaps and widths. Every action stays a button; no
          variant yet turns one into a link. Focus is one proposal shared by A
          to D: the edge of the cut shape lights pale, in place of the ring
          inside it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether the call to action is the only thing that reads as lit;
          whether a quiet action still reads as pressable; whether it could be
          taken for a tab; and whether Gauntlet, unavailable in the row, still
          reads as an offer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The states are forced copies, so the strip shows their look and not
          their feel; the live buttons respond to a pointer and to the keyboard
          but do nothing. Motion between states, the selected state, the
          horizontal strip, touch, and the call to action's own held, focused
          and unavailable looks are not in this round.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionMaterials021 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        A / Pane is taken forward. It is the closest to the language the tabs,
        toggle and switch already speak, and on the title screen it is the only
        set that reads as balanced and as part of this world. Two things are
        wrong with it as shown: the quiet actions do not stand out enough as
        things to press, and the plain pane is too sleek, not technical enough.
        Its link, its focus and its pointer wash are not accepted; the wash
        reads as blinking. B, C and D are not pursued.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="rest">
      <summary>02.2 / The quiet button at rest</summary>
      <p>
        The pane had two faults: the quiet buttons did not stand out enough as
        things to press, and it was too sleek. Dressing it with marks, or
        replacing it with another kind of object, answered a different question
        and both attempts were discarded. This round changes only what the
        design system already has: the weight of the label and how far the
        surface stands above its background. Each version sits in a slice of the
        title screen and of the Hangar, among the production logomark, tabs,
        switch and call to action.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether the quiet buttons read as things to press beside the tabs and
          the switch, and whether the call to action still leads.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The quiet buttons have no pointer, held, focus or unavailable design
          here; Gauntlet is only dimmed as a stand-in. The ship facts are sample
          values. The tabs and the switch are live.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionRest022 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        Neither change is kept. The heavier label and the extra elevation step
        both look out of place: each bends a rule of the design language, and
        the result reads as a mistake instead of as emphasis. The quiet button
        keeps the regular label and the surface of 02.1. The lesson reaches past
        this round: the quiet button cannot be improved by working against the
        constraints of the language. Something has to be added to it.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="edges">
      <summary>02.3 / A partial edge</summary>
      <p>
        If something is added to the pane, it should come from the instruments
        of a ship and not from web pages. This round tries an edge that is
        deliberately incomplete: drawn around part of the pane and left open
        elsewhere, the way a display frames a reading or a sight frames a
        target. It is dim and never lime, so the call to action stays the one
        lit shape. A partial edge is new to the design system; if one is kept it
        becomes part of the language and is recorded in the scope.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether the quiet buttons now read as equipment and as things to
          press; whether an edge reads as a ship's instrument or as a web
          border; how five framed shapes sit in the title column; and whether
          the frame separates a button from the tabs above the launch row.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The quiet buttons have no pointer, held, focus or unavailable design
          here; Gauntlet is only dimmed as a stand-in. The ship facts are sample
          values. The tabs and the switch are live.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionEdge023 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        1 / Brackets is kept as the working hypothesis. The incomplete edge is a
        technical detail that does not come from web pages, and it makes the
        quiet buttons read as things to press. Two reservations go with it: the
        edge is too bright as drawn, and it stands a little apart from the call
        to action, which has no such frame; the second is accepted for now.
        Heavy sides and the stand-off frame are not pursued.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="tone">
      <summary>02.4 / How bright a bracket is</summary>
      <p>
        The brackets should be a detail that is found on a second look, not the
        first thing seen. Their shape is held still here, the same arms and the
        same gap as in 02.3, and only their colour changes: fainter, in the
        pane's own hue, or in a dim trace of the brand colour.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          At what strength the brackets stop dominating and still read as a
          frame; whether a tone holds on both the panel and the void; and
          whether dim lime on four quiet buttons takes anything from the call to
          action.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The length of the arms and the width of the gap are not varied. The
          quiet buttons have no pointer, held, focus or unavailable design here;
          Gauntlet is only dimmed as a stand-in. The ship facts are sample
          values.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionBracket024 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        2 / The pane's hue is kept. A bracket in a lighter tone of the surface's
        own violet belongs to the pane instead of sitting on it, and it holds on
        both the panel and the void. It may still be too bright. The fainter
        neutral and the dim brand colour are not pursued.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="lightness">
      <summary>02.5 / Bright enough to see, dim enough to recede</summary>
      <p>
        The bracket has to be bright enough to be seen and dim enough to fall
        into the background. This round is a fine adjustment of one number. The
        hue and the chroma of 02.4 are held, and the lightness steps down from
        40% by five at a time. For scale, the pane itself is 11% light on the
        void and 16.5% on a panel. The steps stand side by side so that
        neighbours can be compared.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          The lowest step at which the brackets still read as a frame on the
          panel, where the pane is lighter, and the highest at which they recede
          on the void; and whether one step serves both.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit five in a row, so their buttons
          are narrower than in the game. Arm length and gap are not varied. The
          quiet buttons have no pointer, held, focus or unavailable design here;
          Gauntlet is only dimmed as a stand-in.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionBracketTone025 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        25% is chosen. The bracket is <code>lch(25% 32 290)</code>: the pane's
        hue, visible on the void and close to the surface on a panel. With it
        the quiet button at rest is settled: the pane, a regular label, and a
        1px bracket around each end that leaves the middle 40% of the top and
        bottom open.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="pointer">
      <summary>02.6 / Under a pointer</summary>
      <p>
        A pointer has to get an answer, and the answer should be calm: in 02.1 a
        lime wash over the whole pane read as blinking. The brackets give the
        button a part of its own that can answer instead. Four answers follow.
        The first is what the tabs and the toggle already do, as the reference;
        the other three move or light the brackets and leave the pane alone.
        Each change eases over the interface's short duration.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Move a pointer down a column and along a row. Whether the answer is
          noticed without flicker; whether it belongs to this ship; whether lime
          under a pointer competes with the call to action; and whether a button
          should answer differently from the tabs above it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit four in a row. Touch has no
          pointer state. Held, focus and unavailable are not designed here;
          Gauntlet is only dimmed and does not answer.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionPointer026 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        2 / Brackets light is chosen. Under a pointer the brackets turn lime and
        keep their gap; the pane and the label do not change. The tabs' tint and
        the closing brackets, lit or unlit, are not used for the pointer.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="held">
      <summary>02.7 / Held down</summary>
      <p>
        Buttons have no held look today. A press should be felt as taken before
        the screen changes, and a held button is already pointed at, so its
        brackets are already lit. The question is what more it shows. Four
        answers follow, from nothing to the whole pane lit.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Press and hold the live buttons. Whether the press is felt; whether
          the step from pointed at to held is clear but not loud; and whether a
          held quiet button can be mistaken for the call to action.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit four in a row. A real press is
          brief, and the action that follows it is not shown. Touch is not
          checked. Focus and unavailable are not designed here; Gauntlet is only
          dimmed and does not answer.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionHeld027 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        2 / Pane lights is chosen. While a quiet button is held, its pane takes
        the faint lime tint a tab shows under a pointer, inside the lit
        brackets. The closing brackets and the full lime fill are not used.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="focus">
      <summary>02.8 / Keyboard focus</summary>
      <p>
        The game is played from the keyboard first, so focus has to be found at
        a glance on any button, the call to action included. Today's ring is
        drawn inside the button because the button clips itself to its cut shape
        and would cut off anything outside. It doubles the old outline, and on
        the lime call to action a pale ring barely shows. Three answers follow
        the reference. Two use the bracket the quiet button now has; the third
        stands outside the button and so is the same on every button.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Press Tab through a column and a row. Whether focus is found at once
          on a quiet button and on the call to action; whether it can be told
          from a pointer; and whether a quiet button and the call to action
          should show focus the same way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit four in a row. The call to action
          here is a copy rebuilt without clipping; a frame outside the button
          needs the production button rebuilt the same way, which is a readiness
          question. The tabs and switch keep their own focus. Unavailable is not
          designed; Gauntlet is only dimmed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionFocus028 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        1 / As a pointer is chosen. Keyboard focus is the keyboard's pointer: a
        focused quiet button lights its brackets lime, exactly as under a
        pointer. The call to action, which has no brackets at rest, shows dark
        ones just inside its fill. Today's ring, the closing frame and the sight
        are not used, so the production button does not need rebuilding for a
        frame outside its shape.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="unavailable">
      <summary>02.9 / Unavailable</summary>
      <p>
        An unavailable action keeps its place, so the screen does not rearrange,
        but it must not read as an offer. Today every button fades to 45%, the
        call to action included: the shape, the outline and the lime all stay,
        only weaker, and in a row the result still looks like something to
        press. The game has two cases: one action out of several, as Gauntlet in
        the Hangar; and a whole set, as when a ship cannot launch or the title
        screen is still loading.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether Gauntlet stops reading as an offer and still holds its place;
          whether a whole row that cannot launch reads as one switched-off
          instrument; and whether an unavailable call to action should stay
          recognisably the call to action.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit four in a row. Why an action is
          unavailable is told by the text beside it, not by the button, and is
          not shown. Both buttons are scoped copies.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionUnavailable029 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        1 / Empty frame is chosen, with one change for the call to action. An
        unavailable quiet button loses its pane and keeps its dim brackets
        around a muted label, as an unavailable tab keeps its frame. An
        unavailable call to action loses its fill and keeps brackets in the
        brand lime, so it stays recognisable as the call to action. One
        consequence to watch: lime brackets are also what a quiet button shows
        under a pointer; there the pane and a pale label remain, here neither
        does.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="together">
      <summary>02.10 / How far apart</summary>
      <p>
        The button is settled in every state, so the question turns to buttons
        beside each other. Today the title screen's group leaves 8px between
        buttons and the Hangar's launch row, built separately, leaves 20px. The
        brackets change what a gap does: each button now carries its own frame,
        and the distance between two frames decides whether a set reads as one
        instrument or as separate controls. Widths are held as they are: the
        title screen's buttons fill their group and the Hangar's are 180px each.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether one gap can serve both a column and a row; whether the set
          reads as one piece of equipment; and whether the call to action still
          stands apart from its neighbours when they are close.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The title panels are narrowed to fit four in a row. The horizontal
          strip of Settings and the Leaderboard, and the padding around a set,
          are not in this round. Touch targets are 44px tall in every version;
          whether a 4px gap is comfortable under a finger is not checked.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionTogether0210 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        The gap depends on direction. Buttons stacked top to bottom stand 8px
        apart, as the title screen's group already does. Buttons set start to
        end stand 16px apart, so the Hangar's launch row closes from 20px to
        16px. One gap for both directions is not used.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="strip">
      <summary>02.11 / The surface behind a row</summary>
      <p>
        The Leaderboard and Settings put their buttons on a strip that paints
        its own band of the void. The band was made for the outlined button,
        whose interior is the void: on a panel it gave the outline a matching
        ground. A pane needs no such ground, because it steps up from whatever
        it stands on. On the void page of Settings the band is invisible, so the
        question shows only inside a panel, as on the Leaderboard.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether the band still earns its place; whether the buttons belong to
          the panel without it; and whether the set needs anything to part it
          from the content above.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The panels are narrowed to fit three in a row, and Return to title
          screen is shortened to fit. The Leaderboard's sort pair, which stands
          on the same kind of strip, is adjacent and left out. Below 580px of
          width the strip stacks its buttons; that is not shown.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionStrip0211 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        0 / Today's band is kept, with its spacing brought into the rhythm of
        the gaps. A row of buttons stands on a band of the void, so a quiet
        button looks the same wherever a row appears. The band is always a
        single flex row. It has 8px above and below and 16px at each end, the
        same one, two and four grid units as the gaps between buttons. How the
        row is distributed is the host's choice: buttons pushed to the two ends,
        as on the Leaderboard, or gathered at one end, as in the Hangar. A band
        never wraps or stacks; a row that does not fit holds too many buttons.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="links">
      <summary>02.12 / Links</summary>
      <p>
        A link is an action written into words: it goes somewhere, and it has no
        shape of its own. The game has almost none today, so every way out of a
        screen is a button. The stylesheet's link button is lime words that turn
        pale under a pointer, used only in the Lab. Three materials follow. Each
        is shown in a sentence on a panel and on the void, and standing alone in
        the title screen's group, where CREDITS takes the place of a fifth
        button so that a link can be seen beside buttons. Focus follows the rule
        of 02.8: a focused link looks as it does under a pointer.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          Whether a link is found in a sentence without shouting; whether it
          reads as a quieter kind of action than a button; whether lime words
          compete with the call to action; and whether a link standing alone in
          a set of buttons belongs there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The sentences are sample wording. Whether any action in the game
          becomes a link, CREDITS included, is not decided by this round. Links
          in the Lab's and the studies' prose are not shown. The panels are
          narrowed to fit three in a row.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionLinks0212 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        1 / Lime, underlined is chosen. A link has to look like a link, even in
        this interface: words in the brand colour on a line. Two details are not
        settled: the line should probably be dimmer than shown, and the pointer
        answer is not yet accepted. A link standing among buttons, as CREDITS
        does here, is meant to look a little out of place; whether any action
        becomes one is still not decided.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <details id="line">
      <summary>02.13 / A link's line</summary>
      <p>
        Two fine adjustments, shown together so they can be chosen together. The
        four columns step the line's strength at rest down from the 40% of 02.12
        to 10%. Under each, the same link is held in three pointer answers that
        start from that column's line. The words stay lime throughout. A fourth
        specimen proposes an unavailable link: muted words and no line.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to look for.</strong>
          The faintest line that still marks the words as a link in a sentence
          and on CREDITS standing alone; then which pointer answer is noticed
          from that starting point without becoming loud.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What this cannot show.</strong>
          The live links answer a pointer in the first way only. A held link and
          a visited link are not shown; neither has a design. The panels are
          narrowed to fit four in a row and the sentences are sample wording.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ActionLinkLine0213 client:load />
      <h3>Result</h3>
      <p>
        The line is 20% and the pointer answer is a. A link is lime words on a
        lime line at 20% strength, one pixel thick; under a pointer, and on
        keyboard focus, the line lights to full lime and the words do not
        change.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="whole">02.14 / The whole action</h2>
    <p>
      Every item on the list is settled. This round changes nothing; it puts the
      decisions together so they can be seen as one thing and checked against
      each other.
    </p>
    <ol class="ledger">
      <li>
        <strong>The surface.</strong>
        The pane: one elevation step above a panel, two above the void.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>At rest.</strong>
        A regular label and a 1px bracket around each end, open across the
        middle 40% of the top and bottom, in <code>lch(25% 32 290)</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Under a pointer.</strong>
        The brackets turn lime and keep their gap.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Held down.</strong>
        The pane takes a 28% lime tint inside the lit brackets.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keyboard focus.</strong>
        As a pointer. The call to action shows dark brackets inside its fill.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unavailable.</strong>
        An empty frame with a muted label; the call to action's frame is lime.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Together.</strong>
        8px top to bottom, 16px start to end. A row stands on a band of the
        void, always one flex row, with 8px above and below and 16px at each
        end.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Links.</strong>
        Lime words on a lime line at 20%; the line lights under a pointer and on
        focus.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Four things were never put to a choice. They are shown here as proposals,
      marked in yellow, so the set is complete; each stands unless it is
      objected to.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The call to action held down.</strong>
        Proposed: its fill dims a step. It brightens under a pointer today, so
        dimming is the press.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A link held down.</strong>
        Proposed: no more than under a pointer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An unavailable link.</strong>
        Proposed: muted words and no line.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The selected mark.</strong>
        Proposed: actions lose it. An action commands; what is selected belongs
        to the tabs, the toggle and the switch. The Leaderboard's sort pair, its
        only user in the game, is the adjacent question named in 01.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      One open item of 01 ends without a change: which actions are buttons and
      which are links. Nothing in the game becomes a link. A link is for words
      inside a sentence; a link standing among buttons looks out of place, as
      CREDITS did in 02.12.
    </p>
    <ActionSheet0214 client:load />
    <h3>Result</h3>
    <p>
      The four proposals stand. The sheet above is the accepted design and the
      reference for what follows: every value in it is settled, and nothing in
      part 02 remains open.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="readiness">03 / What the design asks of the code</h2>
    <p>
      The accepted design can be built on the shared button as it stands. No
      part of it needs the button rebuilt, and nothing has to return to
      exploration. It does reach further than the stylesheet: into a test that
      asserts the old behaviour, into Lab tools that lean on a rule the design
      removes, and into the examples of earlier studies. Seven choices are left
      for the scope to settle; none of them is a design question.
    </p>

    <h3>What can be used as it is</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The button's shape.</strong>
        The shared button clips itself to its cut shape. Brackets, the dark
        focus brackets on the call to action and the empty frame are all drawn
        inside that shape, so the clipping stays. A frame outside the button
        would have forced a rebuild; 02.8 did not choose one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The call to action</strong>
        at rest and under a pointer, the 44px target and the label size.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The column.</strong>
        The shared vertical group already has 8px gaps. The title screen, pause
        and run complete use it and need no change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The values.</strong>
        8px and 16px are steps of the spacing scale. The held tint is the mix
        the tabs and the toggle already use under a pointer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The markup of the game.</strong>
        Six game files use the outlined button. If the class keeps its name,
        none of them changes for the button itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Lab page pattern.</strong>
        Tabs already have a page of their own under the design-system group; the
        actions page follows it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What has to change</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The outlined button's rules</strong>
        are replaced: a pane in place of an outline around the void, brackets,
        and the pointer, held, focus and unavailable looks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A button must know what it stands on.</strong>
        A pane is one step above a panel and two above the void. Nothing tells a
        control its ground today; the tabs, toggle and switch use one surface
        everywhere. A panel and a band each need to say what stands on them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One new colour</strong>
        for the brackets. It is not needed on canvas, so the canvas mirror and
        its contract check are untouched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Focus and unavailable for buttons</strong>
        leave the shared inset ring and the shared fade. Both stay in use by the
        tabs, toggle and switch, so their tokens remain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The call to action</strong>
        gains a held look, focus brackets and an empty lime frame.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The strip becomes the band:</strong>
        8px above and below, 16px at the ends and between buttons, always one
        row. Its stacking below 580px and the frame that measures it go. The
        Hangar's launch row, built separately today, adopts the band and closes
        from 20px to 16px.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The link button</strong>
        takes the link's line, and gains an unavailable look.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The selected rule</strong>
        on buttons is removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Lab.</strong>
        A new canonical page for buttons and links; the Inputs page loses its
        button section; the Lab's page list and the design system's list of
        canonical pages follow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The design-system spec.</strong>
        Its button section is rewritten to the accepted design. Most of that
        section already describes buttons that no longer exist.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One browser test.</strong>
        The button test checks that focus draws an outline and that the strip
        becomes a stacked grid at 600px. Both are behaviours the design removes,
        so the test is rewritten to the new contract. The title screen's checks
        of the 44px target and label size still hold.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What it touches beyond buttons</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Lab tools that use a button as a switch.</strong>
        The effects bench, the mount planner and two catalogue examples mark a
        pressed button with the selected rule. Without it they show no state.
        They become switches or toggles in the same change, or the change breaks
        them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Leaderboard's sort pair</strong>
        keeps working, because it swaps the filled and quiet buttons itself. It
        still shows two lime fills at once, which the new quiet button makes
        more obvious. It remains the adjacent question.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lab buttons with no variant.</strong>
        Three benches use a variant that has no rule and three use the bare
        button. They need to name the quiet button or the call to action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Earlier studies.</strong>
        About twenty-five earlier study files use the outlined button from the
        shared stylesheet, the accepted button study among them. Changing the
        shared rule changes what those studies show. Their examples have to be
        frozen first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The red button</strong>
        is drawn as an outline, like the old quiet button, and exists only in
        the Lab. This study does not settle it, so it would be the one button
        left in the old language.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Risks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Focus is thin.</strong>
        On a quiet button, focus is a one-pixel lime line, and it is the same as
        a pointer by design. On the call to action it is two pixels, dark on
        lime. The game is played from the keyboard first. This was chosen with
        the alternatives in view; it has not been measured against any focus
        visibility guideline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two looks are close.</strong>
        An unavailable call to action and a quiet button under a pointer both
        show lime brackets. They differ in pane and label.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Touch.</strong>
        A finger has no pointer state, so the held tint is the only answer a
        touch gets. Nothing in this study was checked on a touch screen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Easing the brackets' colour</strong>
        needs a registered custom property. Browsers without it change the
        colour at once, which is acceptable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A row that never wraps.</strong>
        The Hangar's four buttons at 180px each need 800px. Today that row wraps
        below 1000px.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Left for the scope to settle</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Whether the quiet button keeps the outlined button's class name.</li>
      <li>How a panel and a band tell a button what it stands on.</li>
      <li>How earlier studies keep the old outlined button.</li>
      <li>What the Lab's switch-like buttons become.</li>
      <li>Whether the red button is left alone or removed.</li>
      <li>
        Whether links in the prose of the Lab and the studies take the new line.
      </li>
      <li>
        What the Hangar's row does below 800px: narrower buttons, or a row that
        is allowed to be too wide there.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="scope">04 / Five slices</h2>

    <aside class="principles" aria-labelledby="principles">
      <h3 id="principles">Two proposed changes to the design principles</h3>
      <p>
        Neither is made by this study. Each needs explicit agreement before
        <code>docs/design.md</code>
        changes, and the slices below do not depend on either.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Brand: what lime means.</strong>
          The principles give lime two jobs: the brand, and what can be acted on
          or is selected. An unavailable call to action keeps a lime frame,
          which is neither. Either the principle gains a line, that lime also
          keeps the place of the call to action while it is unavailable, or the
          design-system spec records this as the one exception. Recommended: the
          exception in the spec, and no change to the principles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Art direction: controls belong to a console.</strong>
          This study worked from an intent the principles do not state: the
          interface reads as the lit panes and frames of one ship's console, and
          not as web buttons. It decided two rounds of exploration. Recommended:
          add it to the art direction as one sentence, so the next control
          starts from it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <p>
      The work is cut so that each slice can be delivered and checked on its
      own, and so that the game never shows a half-changed screen. The seven
      choices left by part 03 are settled here as recommendations; each is
      marked where it is used.
    </p>

    <h3>The choices, settled</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The quiet button gets a new class name.</strong>
        The old outlined rules stay under the old name, untouched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Earlier studies keep the old name,</strong>
        and so keep the outlined button exactly as they show it. One decision
        answers both choices: nothing is copied and no study file changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A surface says what stands on it</strong>
        through one custom property: the page default is the void's step, a
        panel raises it, a band sets it back. Only buttons read it. The tabs,
        toggle and switch are not changed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Lab's switch-like buttons</strong>
        become the switch where they turn one thing on or off, and the toggle
        where they choose between two.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The red button is left alone:</strong>
        its rule stays, it is not shown on the actions page, and the spec says
        it is unsettled.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Links in Lab and study prose take the new line,</strong>
        so there is one link.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Below 800px the Hangar's four buttons share the row</strong>
        and grow narrower than 180px. The row stays one row.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The slices</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Slice</th>
            <th scope="col">What it delivers</th>
            <th scope="col">Touches</th>
            <th scope="col">Size</th>
            <th scope="col">Needs</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">1 / The language</th>
            <td>
              The quiet button in every state under its new class; the call to
              action's held, focus and unavailable looks; the link; the bracket
              colour and the ground property. A canonical page for actions shows
              all of it. The game's call to action changes at once in focus,
              held and unavailable; its quiet buttons do not change yet.
            </td>
            <td>
              Shared button styles and tokens; the surface styles; a new Lab
              page and the Lab's page list; the design-system spec's button
              section and list of canonical pages.
            </td>
            <td>Medium</td>
            <td>Nothing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">2 / The game</th>
            <td>
              Every quiet action in the game becomes the new button: the title
              menu, the Hangar's launch row and Remove fitting, pause and run
              complete, the return links on Settings and Credits, the
              Leaderboard's Refresh and sort pair.
            </td>
            <td>
              Six game files, one class name each. The game specs that describe
              those screens' buttons, where they name the outline.
            </td>
            <td>Small</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">3 / The band</th>
            <td>
              The strip becomes the band: one row, 8px above and below, 16px at
              the ends and between buttons, no stacking. The Hangar's launch row
              adopts it and shares its width below 800px. Columns are unchanged.
            </td>
            <td>
              Shared group styles; the Hangar, Settings and Leaderboard footers;
              the button browser test's stacking check; the design-system spec.
            </td>
            <td>Small</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">4 / The Lab</th>
            <td>
              Lab tools use the new button. Buttons with no variant name one.
              Switch-like buttons become switches and toggles, and the selected
              rule on buttons is removed. The Inputs page loses its button
              section, and the button browser test moves to the actions page and
              its new contract.
            </td>
            <td>
              About fifteen Lab components; the Inputs page; the button browser
              test; the Lab spec.
            </td>
            <td>Medium</td>
            <td>1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">5 / One link</th>
            <td>
              Links in the prose of the Lab and the studies take the link's
              line. The old link button rule and the strip's measuring frame are
              removed once nothing uses them.
            </td>
            <td>Shared type and button styles; the study and Lab layouts.</td>
            <td>Small</td>
            <td>1, 3, 4</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Slices 2, 3 and 4 can be taken in any order after 1. Between slices the
      game is consistent: after 1 its quiet buttons are still the old outline
      everywhere; after 2 they are the new button everywhere.
    </p>

    <h3>Checks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Correctness, each slice.</strong>
        The repository's lint, type, contract and browser gates pass. The title
        screen's existing checks of the 44px target and label size keep passing
        unchanged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Correctness, the new contract.</strong>
        The button browser test is rewritten to relations, not values: a focused
        button looks different from the same button at rest; an unavailable
        button does not change under a pointer; a band stays one row at a narrow
        width and the page does not scroll sideways; with reduced motion nothing
        eases.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fidelity.</strong>
        The actions page is compared, state by state, with the sheet in 02.14,
        which holds the accepted values. After slice 2 the title screen and the
        Hangar's launch row are compared with the same sheet's column and row.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Earlier studies.</strong>
        After slice 1 the accepted button study and one other study that uses
        the outlined button render as before.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Still provisional</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Focus on a quiet button is the same one-pixel line as a pointer. It is
        accepted as designed and unmeasured.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing in this design has been seen on a touch screen. A finger gets
        only the held tint.
      </li>
      <li>
        The proposals of 02.14 were accepted as a set, without alternatives: the
        call to action held, a link held, an unavailable link, and no selected
        mark.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Outside this scope</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The Leaderboard's sort pair becoming a toggle. It keeps working and
        keeps showing two lime fills.
      </li>
      <li>The red button.</li>
      <li>
        The tabs, toggle and switch learning what they stand on, as buttons now
        do.
      </li>
      <li>
        The flight deck's controls and the Hangar's mount and part controls,
        which are not actions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This scope sizes the work. It does not start it; implementation needs its
      own go-ahead.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
